sub("""- ASSUMPTION (label, not the user's words): "token sessions" means the BOUND desktop session (the person at the desktop), not an agent holding the same token by bearer. The bearer path keeps its answers (contact detail and timeline refused as today), which is the conservative reading: the opt-in exists to control what an AGENT may read, and the user's earlier Q7 answer ("Open it now") for contact detail by bearer tokens is a separate slice S1e-b. Widening to bearer is one flag in the host-door waiver set if main reads the answer that way. Source: inferred: from the user answers above.""",
"""- ASSUMPTION A1 (label, not the user's words): "token sessions" means the BOUND desktop session, not a bearer call with the same token. Bearer keeps today's answers in every switch state. This is NOT a claim that a bound session is a person: a session is started by presenting the token (`POST /v1/me/desktop-session`), so an agent holding a `desktop_signin` + `read` + `crm` token can start one and read timelines through it while its bearer calls are refused. The slice accepts that because the user's answer is about what a token session sees, and a bound session is the only carrier the app has; it is bounded by revoke, the ledger, the owner's `crm:read`, the org opt-out (D6) and the exclusion of operator tokens (D1). If main reads the answer as also covering bearer tokens (the user's earlier Q7 "Open it now" for contact detail), that is slice S1e-b; if main wants neither, W4 is a one-key removal. Source: inferred: from the user answers above.
- ASSUMPTION A2 (label, not the user's words): "on by default" means a default with an org-level off switch, not an unconditional grant: the org entitlement override `userTokens.contactTimelines`, set to exactly `false`, turns both routes off for bound sessions of that org (404 `contact_timelines_disabled` for a member); unset or true is on. No settings screen is built here (a ticket follows); the override is set through the existing entitlement-override mechanism. Source: inferred: from answer:baab765b-d85c-46b4-b7b9-588eb912c770/419b4bba-4ef1-496b-8239-b0a9b68d561e "Yes, on by default".
- A3: the contact-detail half of the claim (clicking a person shows the record) rests on the user's earlier Q7 answer "Open it now" (answer:baab765b-d85c-46b4-b7b9-588eb912c770/c75dac6b), which main assigned to the bearer slice; this slice applies it to the bound door as the page the user pointed at needs both routes. Source: inferred: from c75dac6b.""")

sub("""D1. A named waiver W4 for the bound door only, pinned by test, in `lib/desktop-token-session.ts`: `GET /v1/orgs/:p/crm/contacts/:p` (contact detail) and `GET /v1/orgs/:p/crm/contacts/:p/timeline` are added to the delegated table with abilities `crm` + `read`; for these two routes the gate treats the route as an ordinary token route (not session-only, not a session action): `signedInPersonOnly` is waived and the session-action refusal and the org opt-in requirement do not apply. Every other gate step still runs: ability ceiling, org allow-list (404), org agent-pause, org switches (`crm.api.enabled`), scope, ledger, rate, approval (none for a read).""",
"""D1. A named waiver W4 for the bound DELEGATED door only (never the host door, never bearer), pinned by test: `GET /v1/orgs/:p/crm/contacts/:p` (contact detail) and `GET /v1/orgs/:p/crm/contacts/:p/timeline` are entries of the delegated table (abilities `crm` + `read`) and members of the exact-key set `DESKTOP_SESSION_ONLY_OPENED` in `lib/desktop-token-session.ts`. HEAD maps to GET (existing `methodOf`), so a HEAD on either route is covered the same way; no other method on those paths is opened. For these routes the gate (in `auth-gate.ts`, applied after the bound door check, never touching `nonDelegable` of a future rule except by clearing it for these exact keys) treats the route as an ordinary token route: `signedInPersonOnly` is waived and the session-action refusal and the org opt-in requirement do not apply; the session-action path is never entered (no `req.userTokenSessionAction`, no `user_token.session_action` audit). Operator-kind tokens get NO W4 (an agent-held, non-expiring orchestrator), exactly as they get no host waivers. Every other gate step still runs: ability ceiling, org allow-list (404), org agent-pause, scope, ledger, rate, and the org CRM family switch (D6: it does not run for these routes by itself because they are session-only in the key-family table, so W4 applies `crm.api.enabled` explicitly, answering `crm_api_disabled` for a member org).""")

sub("""D2. The handlers run unchanged under the token-shaped actor: `requireOrgPermission(crm:read)` (owner role), the org of the contact (a contact id of another org is 404), `forTimelineReader` (Gmail mailbox-read entries stay withheld from a token credential) and `timelineWithSubjects` (colleagues' `personal` entries are hidden, 404 by id).""",
"""D2. The handlers run unchanged under the token-shaped actor (delegated door, so `req.auth` carries the token id): `requireOrgPermission(crm:read)` (owner role), `forTimelineReader` (Gmail mailbox-read entries stay withheld from a token credential) and `timelineWithSubjects` (a colleague's `personal` entries are absent; a colleague's `busy_only` entry arrives as "Busy" with no subjects, no join link). Contact detail of another org's contact is 404 (`getContact`); the contact TIMELINE handler does not check the contact, so another org's contact id yields 200 with an empty, org-scoped page and no data (no handler change; the owner's own entries are visible to their own session by design). Both responses (the detail embeds the first timeline page) get the same filtering.""")

